  "prediction": "**$8.2 Billion Merger: IntercontinentalExchange to Acquire New York Stock Exchange**\n\nIn a significant move, IntercontinentalExchange Inc. (ICE) is set to acquire the New York Stock Exchange (NYSE) in an $8.2 billion takeover. The deal, which is expected to be announced as early as Thursday morning, would see ICE offer approximately $33 per share, with two-thirds of that in stock, representing a 37% premium to the NYSE's closing stock price on Wednesday.\n\nThe acquisition underscores the shift in the global market for derivatives, which has surpassed the market for stocks. ICE, a commodities and derivatives trading platform, has been in talks with NYSE Euronext, the owner of the NYSE, for several months. The deal would link NYSE Euronext to one of the industry's fastest-growing exchanges, ICE, which has some of the highest profit margins in the business.\n\nThe merger would sharply expand ICE, which has a little more than 1,000 employees, while NYSE Euronext has 3,077 employees. The combined company would own 14 stock and futures exchanges and five clearing operations, making it the largest exchange operator in the world.\n\nThe takeover marks a significant shift in the financial industry, as electronic trading continues to gain traction over traditional \"open outcry\" floor trading. The deal would also seal the triumph of derivatives trading over stock trading, which has become a less attractive business in recent years.\n\nNYSE Euronext's businesses in the over-the-counter trading of derivatives, including the Liffe market in London, appear to be the main attraction in the merger talks. The company's electronic Arca platform accounts for 12% of all stock trading, while the NYSE itself is responsible for only 11%.\n\nThe deal is expected to face regulatory scrutiny, but it may pose fewer problems than previous mergers due to the fact that ICE focuses on commodities like oil, natural gas, and cotton, while NYSE Euronext plies mainly in stock and stock options and derivatives.\n\nShares of NYSE Euronext rose more than 21% in after-hours trading, to $29.20, after The Wall Street Journal reported news of the talks. The deal is expected to be approved by regulators and shareholders, paving the way for the creation of a powerful trans-Atlantic company with a significant market share in the trading of stocks and derivatives.",
